"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Favorable findings include a low surgical grade (ISUP grade group 2), negative surgical margins, and no invasion of the seminal vesicles. The absence of lymphovascular invasion is also a positive finding. Countervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 5.3 and the documented biopsy finding of a Gleason 3+4 pattern. Furthermore, the absence of lymph node removal means nodal status is unknown, which widens the uncertainty regarding potential regional spread. The difference between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, though the surgical pathology reported a Gleason 3+4. Postoperative PSA and its trajectory are critical missing pieces of information that would significantly revise this assessment. The current horizon suggests a follow-up period, but the uncertainty in nodal status and postoperative PSA trajectory limits definitive prediction. The fixed estimated horizon is 72.27 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 46.97–97.56 months; its coverage has not been validated."
